WEBVTT
Kind: subtitles
Language: en

1
00:02.730 --> 00:12.190
When you go from synthesis to recommendation, you're going from
“what is the so what of your analysis?” to “what are you actually recommending?”

2
00:12.650 --> 00:19.040
When you develop the recommendation, you want to think about:
is it appropriate for the organisation?

3
00:19.370 --> 00:22.980
Is it actionable? How do you actually make this happen?

4
00:23.430 --> 00:29.890
So let's use an example. Think about the question
of entering a new market with a new product.

5
00:30.210 --> 00:38.080
The synthesis of your analysis could be:
“We should enter this market with this product, for the following reasons.”

6
00:38.410 --> 00:48.770
As you develop the recommendation, you think through what needs
to occur — what actions you need to take for that to become true.

7
00:49.130 --> 00:55.651
Who are the stakeholders you need buy-in from?
What resources may you need to pull together?

8
00:55.651 --> 01:01.250
And among the different approaches to making it happen,
you want to think about

9
01:01.640 --> 01:05.440
which is most appropriate, or best, for your organisation.

10
01:06.020 --> 01:13.630
One tip for making really good recommendations
is to carve out the appropriate time to do it well.

11
01:14.150 --> 01:22.275
I've often seen people spend a lot of time on the analysis
and get to a pretty good synthesis of the “so what”,

12
01:22.275 --> 01:28.350
but not carve out enough time to figure out
how to get to the best recommendation —

13
01:28.490 --> 01:37.990
and how to involve all the different stakeholders, to make sure
the recommendation is as actionable and appropriate as it can be.
